<p>UK lawmakers are pressing the government to publish confidential documents tied to Andrew Mountbatten-Windsor’s former appointment as a UK trade envoy, as scrutiny continues over his links to Jeffrey Epstein and the political fallout around them. Several outlets say MPs backed a motion calling for the material’s release, casting it as a straightforward transparency step amid questions about how Andrew was vetted and what he did in the role. The debate has also broadened beyond Andrew himself. Coverage links the renewed push to fresh attention after U.S. authorities released large batches of Epstein-related files, and to UK police activity involving other public figures mentioned in reporting. Some reports also connect the motion to a wider argument over whether Parliament’s rules and conventions on discussing the Royal Family should change, with supporters saying more open scrutiny could help rebuild public trust in public institutions.</p><img src='https://kagiproxy.com/img/39kwSlJgNuvutI_FhRQ-B6nA43yDt-_FcSskE0BRIELuRMMbQ6BqShv5GhIlh-8QwvitiVEBffrdYNbtI9suwpGtCpYx9kH77xmSycIIBgz2LkBkid9ZA5YDUl2-NsaS6t2NZ31zx-aOVwVdl84PeTUwOhft3PDS8zAoN0qtSowP3Wg12wnxAC8Y8j31xgzGpTPxyLR-Kz5P2n0PBgZbRX8AVb02vfbWLMZVQ0vO6dDQQywhEEYOCYaKMn0BuRUkQ9O4olAtzz8CseSoNA' alt='British newspapers report on the arrest of Andrew Mountbatten-Windsor in London, Feb. 20, 2026.' /><br /><h3>Highlights:</h3><ul><li>Parliamentary rules angle: Supporters framed the motion as both a call for disclosure and part of a push to revisit Commons conventions that limit how MPs can criticise or scrutinise the Royal Family.</li><li>Wider inquiry: Some coverage says police questioned a veteran politician as the Epstein-linked investigation widened, adding momentum to demands for disclosure.</li><li>Vetting questions: Reporting focuses on what records officials relied on when appointing Andrew, and what internal guidance or oversight applied during his time as trade envoy.</li><li>Partial release debate: Novara